e non\u2011sunny.  \nTheir point sets are  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}\\cap\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\},\\qquad\nL_{2}\\cap\\mathcal P=\\{(2,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus after using \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) the only uncovered point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is \\((3,1)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 \u2013 Construct a sunny line through the remaining point\n\nWe need a line through \\((3,1)\\) that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nSelect another point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that is already covered, say \\((1,2)\\).  \nThe line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{1-2}{3-1}=-\\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), this line is sunny. Its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac12 (x-3)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac52 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) gives the integer\u2011coefficient form  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}: \\; x+2y = 5 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 \u2013 Verify coverage and distinctness\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(L_{2}\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\);\n* \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((3,1)\\) (by construction) and also \\((1,2)\\) (used to define it).\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the three lines.\n\nThe three lines are distinct because their equations are different:\n\\(x=1\\), \\(x=2\\), and \\(x+2y=5\\).\n\nExactly one line, \\(L_{3}\\), is sunny; the other two are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n---\n\nSince a configuration satisfying all requirements exists, the integer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=1}\n\\]\n\nis attainable for \\(n=3\\)."
