se different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) are\n\\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\).  No three points of \\(S\\) share any of these slopes,\nhence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The non‑sunny line must contain at least two points\n\nIf \\(L_{0}\\) contained at most one point of \\(S\\), the six points would have to be\ncovered by the two sunny lines.  Since each sunny line can cover at most two\npoints, at most four points could be covered – a contradiction.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n|L_{0}\\cap S|\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Possible non‑sunny lines with \\(\\ge 2\\) points of \\(S\\)\n\nChecking the coordinates of the points in \\(S\\) gives the following\nnon‑sunny lines that meet at least two points:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&y=1\\;(A,C,E),\\qquad y=2\\;(B,F),\\\\\n&x=1\\;(A,B,D),\\qquad x=2\\;(C,F),\\\\\n&x+y=3\\;(B,C),\\qquad x+y=4\\;(D,F,E).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe treat each case separately, removing the points on \\(L_{0}\\) and asking\nwhether the remaining points can be covered by two sunny lines (each\ncovering at most two points).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1: \\(L_{0}=y=1\\) (covers \\(A,C,E\\))\n\nRemaining points: \\(B,D,F\\).\nAmong these three, the slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,D)=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(B,F)=0,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(D,F)=-1,\n\\]\n\nall forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence no sunny line can contain two of them,\nso three points would require three sunny lines – impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2: \\(L_{0}=y=2\\) (covers \\(B,F\\))\n\nRemaining points: \\(A,C,E,D\\).\nThe only admissible pair among them is \\(C\\!-\\!D\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).\nThus one sunny line could be \\(CD\\); the two points left are \\(A\\) and \\(E\\),\nwhich are horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) and cannot be placed on a sunny line.\nAgain more than two sunny lines would be needed – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 3: \\(L_{0}=x=1\\) (covers \\(A,B,D\\))\n\nRemaining points: \\(C,E,F\\).\nTheir pairwise slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(C,E)=0,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(C,F)=\\infty,\\;\n\\operatorname{slope}(E,F)=-1,\n\\]\n\nnone admissible.  Hence impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 4: \\(L_{0}=x=2\\) (covers \\(C,F\\))\n\nRemaining points: \\(A,B,D,E\\).\nThe only admissible pair is \\(B\\!-\\!E\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\nAfter using the sunny line \\(BE\\), the points \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) remain;\nthey are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), so they cannot share a sunny line.\nImpossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 5: \\(L_{0}=x+y=3\\) (covers \\(B,C\\))\n\nRemaining points: \\(A,D,E,F\\).\nAdmissible pairs are \\(A\\!-\\!F\\) (slope \\(1\\)) and \\(E\\!-\\!F\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\nIf we choose \\(AF\\), the leftover points \\(D,E\\) have slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden);\nif we choose \\(EF\\), the leftover points \\(A,D\\) are vertical (forbidden).\nThus the four points cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 6: \\(L_{0}=x+y=4\\) (covers \\(D,F,E\\))\n\nRemaining points: \\(A,B,C\\).\nAll pairwise slopes among \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) are forbidden\n(\\(AB\\) vertical, \\(AC\\) horizontal, \\(BC\\) slope \\(-1\\)).\nHence impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, the assumed\nconfiguration cannot exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with exactly }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\n