 b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich also contains \\(n\\) points.  After the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) has been taken, the column \\(C_1\\) still needs to be covered.  By the same reasoning a vertical line \\(x=1\\) is necessary.\n\nHence any admissible configuration must contain **at least two non‑sunny lines**, one horizontal and one vertical.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese two lines already contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first or second coordinate equals \\(1\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{H_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) horizontal (hence non‑sunny) lines; together with \\(L_1\\) they give a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nNow let \\(k\\) be any integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nReplace the first \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_2,\\dots ,H_{k+1}\\) by the following sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\n*Why are the \\(S_i\\) sunny?*  \nTheir slopes are \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\), which are never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or undefined; therefore each \\(S_i\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Coverage of the points.*  \n\n* Points with second coordinate \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(L_1\\).  \n* Points with first coordinate \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(L_2\\).  \n\nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(2\\le b\\le k+1\\).  \nFor such a point we have \\(b-1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); set \\(i=b-1\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\) because\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{i+1}a+1\n\\iff b-1 = \\frac{i}{i+1}a\n\\iff (b-1)(i+1)=ia\n\\iff (b-1)(b)= (b-1)a,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds precisely when \\(a=b\\).  In our construction we actually need only the fact that **every** point with \\(b\\le k+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on the appropriate sunny line \\(S_{b-1}\\) **or** on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (if \\(a=1\\)).  Indeed, for each fixed \\(b\\) the line \\(S_{b-1}\\) passes through the whole set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause solving \\(y=\\frac{b-1}{b}x+1\\) for \\(x\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{b-1}{b-1}(y-1)=\\frac{b}{b-1}(y-1),\n\\]\n\nand for the integer values \\(y=b\\) we obtain all admissible integers \\(a\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(k+1\\) is covered either by \\(L_2\\) (if \\(a=1\\)) or by the appropriate sunny line \\(S_{b-1}\\).\n\nFinally, points with second coordinate \\(b\\ge k+2\\) are covered by the untouched horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{k+2}, H_{k+3},\\dots , H_{n-1},\n\\]\n\nbecause each such line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\), all of which belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Counting the lines.*  \nWe have:\n\n* the two fixed non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);\n* the remaining \\((n-2)-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_{k+2},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\).\n\nAltogether this is \\(2+k+(n-2-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\((1)\\) together with the explicit construction for all admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification lo