ontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) and one sunny line\n   \\(\\ell\\) through the single uncovered point \\((1,n)\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=x+n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  All points are\n   covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   **(iii) \\(k=2\\) (possible for every \\(n\\ge4\\)).**  \n   Choose the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-3}\\) and the diagonal line\n   \\(D_n\\) (which contains the points \\((1,n-1),(2,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,1)\\)).\n   The points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that are still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,n-2),\\;P_2=(3,n-2),\\;P_3=(2,n-1),\\;P_4=(1,n).\n   \\]\n\n   They can be paired by two sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: \\text{through }P_1\\text{ and }P_3\n            &&\\Longrightarrow\\; \\ell_1:\\;y=x+n-3\\quad(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: \\text{through }P_2\\text{ and }P_4\n            &&\\Longrightarrow\\; \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+n+4\\quad(\\text{slope }-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Both slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) are sunny.\n   Altogether we have \\(n-3+1+2=n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which are sunny,\n   and every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) belongs to one of them.\n\n   **(iv) \\(k=3\\) (possible for every \\(n\\ge3\\)).**  \n   Take the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-3}\\) and the three sunny\n   lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: (1,n)\\;\\text{and}\\;(2,n-2) &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-2x+n+4,\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: (1,n-1)\\;\\text{and}\\;(2,n-2) &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y=x+n-2,\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: (3,n-2)\\;\\text{and}\\;(2,n-1) &&\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{n+3}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The three slopes are \\(-2,\\,1,\\,-\\tfrac12\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   consequently the three lines are sunny and distinct.  \n   The horizontal lines cover all points with \\(b\\le n-