"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have solved the problem completely.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines in a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   \\Delta_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\qquad\\bigl(|\\Delta_n|=\\tfrac{n(n+1)}2\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   A *sunny* line is a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Obviously at most \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total. Hence  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A convenient \u201cbase\u201d family.**  \n   For any \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) consider the \\(n-m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-m).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) with ordinate \\(b\\le n-m\\) are all covered by (2).  \n   The *uncovered* points form a right\u2011angled triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{m}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\Delta_n\\mid b>n-m\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{m}|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Covering the top triangle with at most three sunny lines.**  \n   The set \\(T_{3}\\) (the case \\(m=3\\)) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &P_{1}=(1,n),\\;P_{2}=(2,n-1),\\;P_{3}=(3,n-2),\\\\\n   &Q_{1}=(1,n-1),\\;Q_{2}=(2,n-2),\\;Q_{3}=(1,n-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_{1}&:\\;y=-2x+n+2 &&\\text{through }P_{1},\\,P_{2},\\\\\n   \\ell_{2}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{n+2}{2} &&\\text{through }Q_{1},\\,P_{3},\\\\\n   \\ell_{3}&:\\;y= x+n-2 &&\\text{through }Q_{2},\\,Q_{3},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, have slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,1\\) (none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), and together cover all six points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \n   Consequently **any** triangle \\(T_{m}\\) with \\(m\\le 3\\) can be covered by at most three sunny lines (for \\(m<3\\) we simply omit the unnecessary lines among \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\)).\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 choose the size of the top triangle.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   s=\\min\\{k,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Use the \\(n-s\\) horizontal lines (2) with indices \\(1,\\dots ,n-s\\).  \n   The uncovered set is \\(T_{s}\\) (size \\(\\frac{s(s+1)}2\\)).\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 cover the uncovered set with exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(s=0\\) nothing is added.  \n   For \\(s=1,2,3\\) we take the first \\(s\\) lines among \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) defined in (4).  \n   By the discussion in \u00a74 these \\(s\\) sunny lines together cover all points of \\(T_{s}\\).\n\n   At this stage we have used \\(n-s\\) horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines and \\(s\\) sunny lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) lines, with exactly \\(s\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 increase the number of sunny lines from \\(s\\) to the required \\(k\\) (when \\(k>3\\)).*  \n   When \\(k>s\\) we still have \\(k-s\\) \u201cspare\u2019\u2019 lines among the horizontal family (because \\(n-s\\ge n-3\\ge k-s\\) for every \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n   Replace any \\(k-s\\) distinct horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) by **new** sunny lines that pass through a point already covered (so the covering property is preserved).  \n   For each replacement choose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes already used; there are infinitely many such slopes, hence the new lines can be made pairwise distinct.  \n   After the replacement we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\).\n\n   Because the replacement uses only