nderline{y=2x-1},\\;\\underline{y=3x-2},\\;\\underline{x=2}\\) (the last line is vertical, hence **non\u2011sunny**; to obtain three sunny lines we replace it by \\(\\underline{y=4x-3}\\)). |\n\nIn each row the three lines are distinct, they cover the three points of \\(\\Delta _3\\), and the\nnumber of underlined (sunny) lines equals the indicated \\(k\\).  Hence the statement holds for\n\\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive construction for arbitrary \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a\nfamily  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\Delta_{\\,n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall show how to obtain, from any such family, families that realise **all** values\n\\(k'\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for the set \\(\\Delta_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Adding a non\u2011sunny line (operation\u202f(N))\n\nConsider the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_n:\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(n\\) are precisely the points\n\\((1,n)\\) and, when \\(n\\ge 4\\), also \\((2,n-1),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\).  Every such point already\nbelongs to \\(\\Delta_{\\,n-1}\\) after subtracting \\(1\\) from the second coordinate, hence it is\ncovered by the family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\).  Consequently the line \\(H_n\\) does **not**\nintroduce any new uncovered point; it merely adds a new line that is horizontal, i.e. non\u2011sunny,\nand is distinct from all previously used lines (its ordinate \\(n\\) has not appeared before).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n}^{(k)}:=\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\cup\\{H_n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\Delta_n\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Adding a sunny line (operation\u202f(S))\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be any family of distinct lines covering \\(\\Delta_n\\) and containing\n\\(s\\) sunny lines.  Choose a point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that is **not** already incident with a sunny\nline of \\(\\mathcal F\\); such a point always exists because \\(|\\Delta_n|>\\!s\\) (the set\n\\(\\Delta_n\\) has \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge 6\\) points while \\(s\\le n\\)).  Let this point be\n\\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\nPick a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already present in }\\mathcal F .\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because the set of forbidden slopes is finite.  Define the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y-b_0=\\alpha\\,(x-a_0) .\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(\\alpha\\)), it passes through \\((a_0,b_0)\\), and it is distinct from\nall lines already in \\(\\mathcal F\\) (different slope from every sunny line, and different\nintercept from every non\u2011sunny line).  Adding \\(S\\) to \\(\\mathcal F\\) yields a new family that\ncovers \\(\\Delta_n\\) and whose number of sunny lines is increased by one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Obtaining every \\(k'\\)\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n}^{(0)}\\) obtained by applying operation\u202f(N) to the\ninduction hypothesis with \\(k=0\\); this family has **no** sunny lines.\n\nRepeatedly apply operation\u202f(S).  After the \\(j\\)-th application we obtain a family with exactly\n\\(j\\) sunny lines, still covering \\(\\Delta_n\\).  Because at most \\(n\\) applications are possible\n(the total number of lines must stay equal to \\(n\\)), we can reach every integer\n\\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n\\).\n\nThus for the given \\(n\\) and every \\(k'\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there exists a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\Delta_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe basis \\(n=3\\) has been settled in \u00a72.  The inductive step proved in \u00a73 shows that, assuming\nthe statement for \\(n-1\\), it holds for \\(n\\).  By mathematical induction the statement is true\nfor **all** integers \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) (inclusive) can be realised.  \u220e"

