            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and elementary facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta _n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\Delta _n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta _n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1 .\n\\]\n\nAll other slopes are allowed.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\) (basis of induction)\n\n\\[\n\\Delta _3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following six families each consist of three distinct lines, cover the three points,\nand contain exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | family (sunny lines are underlined) |\n|------|--------------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\) |\n| 1 | \\(\\underline{y=2x-1},\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(\\underline{y=2x-1},\\;\\underline{y=3x-2},\\;x=2\\) |\n| 3 | \\(\\underline{y=2x-1},\\;\\underline{y=3x-2},\\;\\underline{y=4x-3}\\) |\n\n*Verification.*  \n- For \\(k=0\\) the three non‑sunny lines clearly contain the three points.  \n- For \\(k=1\\) the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\); the vertical line\n  \\(x=1\\) covers \\((1,2)\\); the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers \\((2,1)\\).  \n- For \\(k=2\\) the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\); the sunny line\n  \\(y=3x-2\\) contains \\((1,2)\\); the vertical line \\(x=2\\) contains \\((2,1)\\).  \n- For \\(k=3\\) each of the three sunny lines passes through a different point of\n  \\(\\Delta _3\\); together they cover all three points.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there\nexists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{\\,n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\Delta_{\\,n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\nWe shall construct, for the same \\(k\\), a family that covers \\(\\Delta_n\\) and then show how\nto increase the number of sunny lines up to \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Adding two non‑sunny lines (operation (N))\n\nConsider the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_n:\\;y=n\n\\]\n\nand the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_n:\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (they are parallel to the axes) and they have never appeared before,\nbecause the only horizontal (resp. vertical) lines used in \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) have\nordinates (resp. abscissae) in \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) belongs to one of the following three disjoint subsets:\n\n* **(i)** points with second coordinate \\(n\\): the unique point \\((1,n)\\);\n* **(ii)** points with first coordinate \\(n\\): the unique point \\((n,1)\\);\n* **(iii)** points with both coordinates \\(\\le n-1\\): these are exactly the points of\n  \\(\\Delta_{\\,n-1}\\).\n\nThus the set \\(\\{H_n,V_n\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines,\ncovers all three subsets, and still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one (operation (S))\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be any family of distinct lines covering \\(\\Delta_n\\) and containing\n\\(s\\) sunny lines.  We claim that there exists a point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that lies on **no**\nsunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nIndeed, a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(\\Delta_n\\) (the maximal case occurs for the line \\(y=x\\), which meets the set in the\npoints \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)).  Consequently the\nunion of the \\(s\\) sunny lines contains at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|\\Delta_n|\n\\qquad\\text{(because }s\\le n\\text{).}\n\\]\n\nHence some point \\(P\\in\\Delta_n\\) is not incident with any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already present in }\\mathcal F .\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many slopes are forbidden, so such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists.  Define the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y-P_y=\\alpha\\,(x-P_x).\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) passes through \\(P\\), has a sunny slope, and is distinct from every line already in\n\\(\\mathcal F\\) (different slope from all sunny lines, and different intercept from any\nhorizontal/vertical line).  Replacing **one** of the two non‑sunny lines \\(H_n\\) or\n\\(V_n\\) by \\(S\\) yields a new family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) that still covers \\(\\Delta_n\\), has the\nsame total number of lines, and whose number of sunny lines is \\(s+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Obtaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nStart with the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0:=\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\cup\\{H_n,V_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n+1\\) lines in total.  \nApply operation (S) repeatedly, each time replacing one of the