e horizontal and cannot be placed together on a sunny line.  \nThus the complement cannot be covered by two sunny lines – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 3.  \\(L:x=1\\)\n\n\\(L\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nComplement: \\(C=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\nAll three possible lines through two points of \\(C\\) have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal).  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\); two sunny lines cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered – impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 4.  \\(L:x=2\\)\n\n\\(L\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nComplement: \\(C=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe only sunny pair in \\(C\\) is \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nAfter using this pair, the remaining points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) are vertical, which is a forbidden direction, so they cannot lie on a sunny line together.  \nThus the complement cannot be covered by two sunny lines – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 5.  \\(L:x+y=3\\)\n\n\\(L\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nComplement: \\(C=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\nAmong the pairs of \\(C\\) the only sunny line is \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nThe two points left, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), have slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden), so they cannot be placed on a sunny line together.  \nHence the complement cannot be covered by two sunny lines – contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 6.  \\(L:x+y=4\\)\n\n\\(L\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nComplement: \\(C=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nEvery line through two points of \\(C\\) has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal).  \nThus a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(C\\); two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered – impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nAll six possible choices for the unique non‑sunny line lead to a contradiction: the remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines. Therefore no arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
