            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is\n**non‑sunny** (parallel to a horizontal, a vertical or a line of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to the uncovered set\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Let the non‑sunny lines be\n\n* \\(t\\) vertical lines \\(x=c\\)   (\\(c\\in X\\)),\n* \\(u\\) horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in Y\\)),\n* \\(v\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\in D\\)),\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\nt+u+v=n-k,\\qquad |X|=t,\\;|Y|=u,\\;|D|=v .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) that are not on any of the non‑sunny lines form the set\n\n\\[\nU_n(X,Y,D)=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\notin X,\\;b\\notin Y,\\;a+b\\notin D\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(U_n\\) must be covered by a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lower bound for \\(|U_n|\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nFor any subsets \\(X,Y,D\\subset\\mathbb Z\\) satisfying \\(|X|+|Y|+|D|=m\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n|U_n(X,Y,D)|\\ge\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the right‑angled triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nRemoving the vertical strips \\(\\{x\\in X\\}\\) deletes at most \\(|X|\\) whole rows of\n\\(T_n\\); similarly the horizontal strips delete at most \\(|Y|\\) whole columns,\nand each diagonal strip \\(\\{x+y\\in D\\}\\) deletes at most one additional row.\nConsequently after deleting all \\(m\\) strips the remaining lattice points form a\ntriangle of side‑length at least \\(n-m\\).  The number of lattice points in a\ntriangle of side‑length \\(s\\) equals \\(s(s+1)/2\\); substituting \\(s=n-m\\) yields\n(7). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  How many sunny lines are necessary?\n\nA sunny line meets each vertical line, each horizontal line and each diagonal\nline in at most one point of \\(S_n\\).  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most\none** point of the set \\(U_n(X,Y,D)\\).  Therefore at least \\(|U_n|\\) sunny\nlines are required to cover all points of \\(U_n\\).\n\nCombining this observation with Lemma 1 and using \\(m=n-k\\) we obtain the\nnecessary inequality\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;|U_n|\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(k)(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Solving the inequality\n\nFor an integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) inequality (8) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;k(k-1)\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the only possibilities are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge n-1\\) we have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) because we have only \\(n\\) lines in\ntotal.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}\\;}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nis a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration, for every\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the inequality (8) gives \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\); the value \\(k=2\\) is\nexcluded.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions showing sufficiency\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of lines for each admissible value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(x=a\\) for some\n\\(i\\); hence the family covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \nThe only point of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  Add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)+1\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\) is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  The resulting \\(n\\) lines are distinct and\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.3  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -i\\,x + (i+1).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) set \\(i=n+1-a-b\\) (note that \\(0\\le i\\le n-1\\)).\nThen \\(b = -i a + (i+1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny\nlines (10) cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.4  \\(k=n-1\\)  (only for \\(n\\ge 4\\))\n\nTake the diagonal non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\).  The remaining points form the\nset \\(S_{n-1}\\).  Apply the construction of §5.3 with \\(n\\) replaced by\n\\(n-1\\); this yields \\(n-1\\) sunny lines that cover \\(S_{n-1}\\).  Together with\n\\(D\\) we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Impossibility of the other values\n\nAssume a configuration exists with a value of \\(k\\) not belonging to the set\n(9).  Then \\(k\\) satisfies \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) (or \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)).\nFor such a \\(k\\) inequality (8) is violated because  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2 > k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently at least \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) sunny lines would be required to cover\nthe uncovered set, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(k\\) sunny lines are\navailable.  Hence no configuration exists for those values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly those listed in (9); for\n\\(n=3\\) this reduces to \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  Explicit families of lines have been\nprovided for each admissible \\(k\\), and a rigorous counting argument shows that\nno other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\qquad\n       \\text{for }n\\ge4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
